    <h1>Out Variable in C#</h1>

    <p>
        The <strong>out</strong> keyword in C# is used to pass arguments by reference.
        It allows a method to return multiple values through method parameters.
    </p>

    <p>
        The variable passed as an <strong>out</strong> parameter does not need to be
        initialized before method call, but the called method must assign a value
        before returning.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Why Use out?</h2>

    <p>
        Normally a method can return only one value using the
        <strong>return</strong> statement.
    </p>

    <p>
        Using <strong>out</strong>, we can return multiple values from a method.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Syntax</h2>

    <pre>
methodName(out variableName);
    </pre>

    <p>Method Definition:</p>

    <pre>
void methodName(out int number)
{
    number = 10;
}
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Basic Example</h2>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void GetNumber(out int num)
    {
        num = 100;
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        int value;

        GetNumber(out value);

        Console.WriteLine(value);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
100
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Returning Multiple Values Using out</h2>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void GetValues(out int a, out int b)
    {
        a = 10;
        b = 20;
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        int x, y;

        GetValues(out x, out y);

        Console.WriteLine("X = " + x);
        Console.WriteLine("Y = " + y);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
X = 10
Y = 20
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Real-world Example - int.TryParse()</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>out</strong> is commonly used with <strong>TryParse()</strong>
        methods for input validation.
    </p>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string number = "123";

        bool result = int.TryParse(number, out int value);

        Console.WriteLine(result);
        Console.WriteLine(value);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
True
123
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>TryParse()</strong> returns true or false.</li>
        <li>The converted integer value is returned using <strong>out</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Difference Between ref and out</h2>

    <table border="1" cellpadding="10">
        <tr>
            <th>ref</th>
            <th>out</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Variable must be initialized before passing</td>
            <td>Initialization is not required</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Used for existing values</td>
            <td>Used for returning values</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Method may or may not assign value</td>
            <td>Method must assign value</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <hr>

    <h2>Important Points</h2>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>out</strong> parameters must be assigned a value inside the method.</li>
        <li>Variables passed with <strong>out</strong> do not require initialization before method call.</li>
        <li>Used to return multiple values from a method.</li>
        <li>Passed by reference.</li>
        <li><strong>out</strong> keyword must be used in both method declaration and method call.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Interview Definition</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>out</strong> keyword in C# is used to pass arguments by reference
        and allows a method to return multiple values. The variable passed as an
        <strong>out</strong> parameter does not need to be initialized before method
        call, but the called method must assign a value before returning.
    </p>

